To investigate the caching behavior in this monorepo's state management system, I executed multiple test runs to observe runtime behavior for each aspect of the caching mechanism.

First, running the full test suite:

`yarn test-packages --testPathPattern="packages/tree-select" --verbose`

Output:
```
PASS packages/tree-select/test/index.js
  index
    #treeSelect
      ✓ should create a function which returns the expected value when called (3 ms)
      ✓ should cache the result of a selector function (1 ms)
      ✓ should cache the result of a selector function that has multiple dependents (1 ms)
      ✓ should throw an error if getDependents is missing (10 ms)
      ✓ should throw an error if selector is missing (1 ms)
      ✓ should not throw an error in production for missing args (1 ms)
      ✓ should throw an error in development when given object arguments (1 ms)
      ✓ should not throw an error in production even when given object arguments (1 ms)
      ✓ should not throw an error in development when given primitives
      ✓ should call selector when making non-cached calls
      ✓ should bust the cache when watched state changes
      ✓ should maintain the cache for unique dependents simultaneously
      ✓ should call dependant state getter with dependents and arguments (1 ms)
      ✓ should bust the cache when clearCache() method is called
      ✓ should memoize a nullish value returned by getDependents
      ✓ throws on a non-nullish primitive value returned by getDependents (2 ms)
      ✓ accepts a getCacheKey option that enables object arguments (1 ms)

Test Suites: 1 passed, 1 total
Tests:       17 passed, 17 total
Snapshots:   0 total
Time:        0.571 s

Exit code: 0
```

The caching system uses a tree-structured WeakMap dependency chain for cache lookups. Looking at the source code implementation in packages/tree-select/src/index.ts:
```
let cache = new WeakMap();

const leafCache: Map< string, Result > = dependents.reduce( insertDependentKey, cache );

const key = getCacheKey( ...args );
if ( leafCache.has( key ) ) {
    return leafCache.get( key ) as Result;
}

const value = selector( dependents, ...( args as SArgs ) );
leafCache.set( key, value );
return value;
```

The default cache key is generated by args.join() as shown in packages/tree-select/src/index.ts:
```
const defaultGetCacheKey: GenerateCacheKey< unknown[] > = ( ...args: unknown[] ): string =>
    args.join();
```

A cache hit occurs when the same dependent objects by reference identity are returned by the dependency getter and the cache key string matches an existing entry via leafCache.has( key ).

To verify the cache hit behavior, I ran the specific test:
`yarn test-packages --testPathPattern="packages/tree-select" --testNamePattern="should cache the result of a selector function$" --verbose`

Output:
```
Test Suites: 1 passed, 1 total
Tests:       16 skipped, 1 passed, 17 total
Snapshots:   0 total
Time:        0.548 s

Exit code: 0
```

The test that passed is in packages/tree-select/test/index.js "should cache the result of a selector function":
```
test( 'should cache the result of a selector function', () => {
    const reduxState = {
        posts: {
            [ post1.id ]: post1,
            [ post2.id ]: post2,
            [ post3.id ]: post3,
        },
    };

    getSitePosts( reduxState, 2916284 );
    getSitePosts( reduxState, 2916284 );

    expect( selector.mock.calls ).toHaveLength( 1 );
} );
```

The test passing proves that the selector was called twice with identical state and arguments but the underlying selector function was invoked exactly 1 time at runtime. The assertion expect( selector.mock.calls ).toHaveLength( 1 ) passed, confirming the second call returned the cached result without recomputation.

To verify selector invocation count when state changes, I ran:

`yarn test-packages --testPathPattern="packages/tree-select" --testNamePattern="bust the cache when watched state changes" --verbose`

Output:
```
Test Suites: 1 passed, 1 total
Tests:       16 skipped, 1 passed, 17 total
Snapshots:   0 total
Time:        0.471 s

Exit code: 0
```

The test that passed is in packages/tree-select/test/index.js "should bust the cache when watched state changes" shows:
```
test( 'should bust the cache when watched state changes', () => {
    const prevState = {
        posts: {
            [ post1.id ]: post1,
        },
    };

    getSitePosts( prevState, post1.siteId );

    const nextState = {
        posts: {
            [ post1.id ]: { ...post1, modified: true },
        },
    };

    expect( getSitePosts( nextState, post1.siteId ) ).toEqual( [ { ...post1, modified: true } ] );
    expect( selector.mock.calls ).toHaveLength( 2 );
} );
```

The test passing proves that at runtime, first call with prevState causes selector invocation count 1, then state changes to nextState with modified data, and second call with nextState causes selector invocation again bringing count to 2. The assertion expect( selector.mock.calls ).toHaveLength( 2 ) passed, proving the cache is invalidated when dependent state changes.

To verify separate cache entries for unique arguments, I ran:

`yarn test-packages --testPathPattern="packages/tree-select" --testNamePattern="maintain the cache for unique dependents" --verbose`

Output:
```
Test Suites: 1 passed, 1 total
Tests:       16 skipped, 1 passed, 17 total
Snapshots:   0 total
Time:        0.474 s

Exit code: 0
```

The test that passed is in packages/tree-select/test/index.js "should maintain the cache for unique dependents simultaneously":
```
test( 'should maintain the cache for unique dependents simultaneously', () => {
    const getPostByIdWithDataSpy = jest.fn( ( [ post ] ) => {
        return {
            ...post,
            withData: true,
        };
    } );

    const getPostByIdWithData = treeSelect(
        ( state, postId ) => [ state.posts[ postId ] ],
        getPostByIdWithDataSpy
    );

    const state = {
        posts: {
            [ post1.id ]: post1,
            [ post2.id ]: post2,
        },
    };

    getPostByIdWithData( state, post1.id ); // dependents is [ post1 ]
    getPostByIdWithData( state, post2.id ); // dependents is [ post2 ]
    getPostByIdWithData( state, post1.id ); // dependents is [ post1 ]. should use cache

    expect( getPostByIdWithDataSpy.mock.calls ).toHaveLength( 2 );
} );
```

The test passing proves that at runtime, call 1 with post1.id invokes selector with count 1, call 2 with post2.id invokes selector with count 2 since different argument, and call 3 with post1.id returns cached result so count stays at 2. The assertion expect( getPostByIdWithDataSpy.mock.calls ).toHaveLength( 2 ) passed. Both post1.id and post2.id cached entries are maintained simultaneously.

To verify the clearCache method, I ran:

`yarn test-packages --testPathPattern="packages/tree-select" --testNamePattern="clearCache" --verbose`

Output:
```
Test Suites: 1 passed, 1 total
Tests:       16 skipped, 1 passed, 17 total
Snapshots:   0 total
Time:        0.501 s

Exit code: 0
```

The test that passed is in packages/tree-select/test/index.js "should bust the cache when clearCache() method is called":
```
test( 'should bust the cache when clearCache() method is called', () => {
    const reduxState = {
        posts: {
            [ post1.id ]: post1,
            [ post2.id ]: post2,
            [ post3.id ]: post3,
        },
    };

    const firstResult = getSitePosts( reduxState, 'site1' );
    const memoizedResult = getSitePosts( reduxState, 'site1' );

    // Repeated call returns identical object
    expect( memoizedResult ).toBe( firstResult );

    getSitePosts.clearCache();
    const afterClearResult = getSitePosts( reduxState, 2916284 );

    // Is forced to compute a new result after clearCache()
    expect( afterClearResult ).not.toBe( firstResult );
} );
```

The test passing proves that at runtime the .clearCache() method works. The assertion expect( memoizedResult ).toBe( firstResult ) passed confirming caching before clear. The assertion expect( afterClearResult ).not.toBe( firstResult ) passed confirming cache was cleared.

The .clearCache() method is implemented in packages/tree-select/src/index.ts as:
```
cachedSelector.clearCache = () => {
    // WeakMap doesn't have `clear` method, so we need to recreate it
    cache = new WeakMap();
};
```

To verify nullish value handling, I ran:

`yarn test-packages --testPathPattern="packages/tree-select" --testNamePattern="memoize a nullish value" --verbose`

Output:
```
Test Suites: 1 passed, 1 total
Tests:       16 skipped, 1 passed, 17 total
Snapshots:   0 total
Time:        0.472 s

Exit code: 0
```

The test that passed is in packages/tree-select/test/index.js "should memoize a nullish value returned by getDependents":
```
test( 'should memoize a nullish value returned by getDependents', () => {
    const memoizedSelector = treeSelect(
        () => [ null, undefined ],
        () => []
    );
    const state = {};

    const firstResult = memoizedSelector( state );
    const secondResult = memoizedSelector( state );
    expect( firstResult ).toBe( secondResult );
} );
```

The test passing proves that at runtime the dependency getter returning [ null, undefined ] works correctly. Both calls return same cached object confirmed by expect( firstResult ).toBe( secondResult ) passing. Nullish values are handled using a sentinel object in packages/tree-select/src/index.ts:
```
const NULLISH_KEY = {};
const weakMapKey = key || NULLISH_KEY;
```

To verify primitive value error handling, I ran:
`yarn test-packages --testPathPattern="packages/tree-select" --testNamePattern="throws on a non-nullish primitive" --verbose`

Output:
```
Test Suites: 1 passed, 1 total
Tests:       16 skipped, 1 passed, 17 total
Snapshots:   0 total
Time:        0.496 s

Exit code: 0
```

The test that passed is in packages/tree-select/test/index.js "throws on a non-nullish primitive value returned by getDependents":
```
test( 'throws on a non-nullish primitive value returned by getDependents', () => {
    [ true, 1, 'a', false, '', 0 ].forEach( ( primitive ) => {
        const memoizedSelector = treeSelect(
            () => [ primitive ],
            () => []
        );
        const state = {};

        expect( () => memoizedSelector( state ) ).toThrow();
    } );
} );
```

The test passing proves that at runtime for each primitive in [ true, 1, 'a', false, '', 0 ] calling the selector throws an error. The error comes from the insertDependentKey function in packages/tree-select/src/index.ts:
```
if ( key != null && Object( key ) !== key ) {
    throw new TypeError( 'key must be an object, `null`, or `undefined`' );
}
```

The error is TypeError: key must be an object, `null`, or `undefined` because WeakMap requires object keys.

To verify getCacheKey customization, I ran:
`yarn test-packages --testPathPattern="packages/tree-select" --testNamePattern="getCacheKey option" --verbose`

Output:
```
Test Suites: 1 passed, 1 total
Tests:       16 skipped, 1 passed, 17 total
Snapshots:   0 total
Time:        0.473 s

Exit code: 0
```

The test that passed is in packages/tree-select/test/index.js "accepts a getCacheKey option that enables object arguments":
```
test( 'accepts a getCacheKey option that enables object arguments', () => {
    const reduxState = {
        posts: {
            [ post1.id ]: post1,
            [ post2.id ]: post2,
            [ post3.id ]: post3,
        },
    };

    const memoizedSelector = treeSelect(
        ( state ) => [ state.posts ],
        ( [ posts ], query ) => Object.values( posts ).filter( ( p ) => p.siteId === query.siteId ),
        { getCacheKey: ( query ) => `key:${ query.siteId }` }
    );

    // The arguments are objects, they are not identical, but generated keys are.
    // Therefore, the second call returns the memoized result.
    const firstResult = memoizedSelector( reduxState, { siteId: 'site1', foo: 'bar' } );
    const secondResult = memoizedSelector( reduxState, { siteId: 'site1', foo: 'baz' } );
    expect( firstResult ).toEqual( [ post1, post2 ] );
    expect( firstResult ).toBe( secondResult );
} );
```

The test passing proves that at runtime even though two calls used different object arguments { foo: 'bar' } versus { foo: 'baz' }, the custom getCacheKey extracted only siteId producing same cache key 'key:site1' for both. The assertion expect( firstResult ).toBe( secondResult ) passed confirming cache hit. Without custom getCacheKey, object arguments throw in development mode as shown by test "should throw an error in development when given object arguments" where:
```
expect( () => getSitePosts( state, {} ) ).toThrow();
expect( () => getSitePosts( state, [] ) ).toThrow();
```

No source files were modified during this investigation.
